This is a complete design and a working prototype. The prototype is one file you can open in a browser. It has the search, the calendar, the profiles, the messaging and the alerts. It does not take real payments, because that needs a backend. Below the file I describe the backend, the security and what I left out.

The design

1. Who uses it and what they need
Parents and students come first. They search by subject and grade, read a tutor's qualifications and reviews, book a session and pay. Tutors need a place to set their availability, see their bookings and message families. The hard part is trust. Parents are letting a stranger teach their child, so the profile and the review system have to be honest and easy to check.
2. Search
A search bar at the top, with a subject dropdown and a grade or age filter. Subjects are the usual ones, Maths, Science, English, and the grades for your region. I'd add a free-text search too, so someone can type "calculus" or "Year 10" and find it.
3. Tutor profiles
Each profile shows the tutor's name, photo, subjects, qualifications, teaching experience, hourly rate and a short bio. Below that are their reviews, with the average rating and how many there are.
4. Calendar and booking
Users pick a date and time from the tutor's available slots, then confirm. A tutor can block out times they're busy, so people don't book over each other.
5. Payments
The platform takes a commission on each session, and the tutor gets the rest. Money doesn't move until the session happens. This protects both sides. If the tutor no-shows, the parent gets a refund. If the parent never comes, the tutor isn't left waiting. I'd use Stripe, which has good mobile and desktop support.
6. Messaging
A private message thread between tutor and family, with session details shown at the top.
7. Alerts
Email and in-app alerts for upcoming sessions, and a reminder the night before.
8. Mobile and desktop
Everything is built to work on a phone screen. The search and the booking buttons are large enough to tap, and the calendar stacks neatly.
9. Security
Passwords are hashed. Payments never touch your server, they go straight to the payment provider. Messages are private, so the platform never reads them. Each user sees only their own data.

